-- Reviews, review photos and the per-user profile — plan sections 9 and 4.1/4.2.
--
-- Generated by drizzle-kit from src/schema/review.ts and src/schema/profile.ts, with ONE
-- statement added by hand at the top. The rules that are not tables — the delivered-order
-- guard, the append-only write guard, the EXIF/erasure paths, `review_is_public()` and the
-- two views — are in 0020_review_guards.sql, the same arrangement 0007 and 0011 use.
--
-- ── The hand-added statement, and why it is not in quote.ts ──────────────────────
--
-- `reviews_line_version_fk` proves two things at once (see the block comment in review.ts):
-- that the catalogue version a review names is that line's own, and — because a `freeform`
-- line has a NULL `product_version_id` and so cannot satisfy a foreign key from a NOT NULL
-- column — that the line is a `catalog` line. A delivery charge is therefore unreviewable
-- by referential integrity rather than by a trigger.
--
-- A composite foreign key needs a UNIQUE on the parent columns, and this one is a constraint
-- on `quote_lines`, a table phase 5c owns. Declaring it in quote.ts would put a constraint
-- that exists solely for this file into that file's list of invariants, where the next
-- reader would have to guess what it is for. It is redundant as a uniqueness claim — `id` is
-- already the primary key — and load-bearing purely as a foreign-key target, which is
-- exactly what `order_events_id_order_key` says about itself in order.ts.
--
-- NULLs are distinct in a unique index by default, so this adds no constraint at all to the
-- free-form lines that carry no version, and it can never fail on existing data.
ALTER TABLE "quote_lines"
  ADD CONSTRAINT "quote_lines_id_product_version_key" UNIQUE ("id", "product_version_id");
